@davidfowl I am using `new Pipe()` with no explicit options ([source](https://github.com/Trolldemorted/VncDotnet/blob/09a48a4012e43a6a29aa841990cd80ce3c5e9828/VncDotnet/Encodings/ZRLEEncoding.cs#L30)).

OK I have a repro. Generally when the length is passed into GetMemory we need to handle the case where's there's an existing segment allocated with nothing written into it, but more space is being requested than is available:

```C#
using System;
using System.IO.Pipelines;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace EmptySegmentsBug
{
    class Program
    {
        static async Task Main(string[] args)
        {
            var pipe = new Pipe();
            
            // Get a new block but don't write anything
            var buffer = pipe.Writer.GetMemory();
            pipe.Writer.Advance(0);
            await pipe.Writer.FlushAsync();

            // Get a new block
            buffer = pipe.Writer.GetMemory(4096 * 2);
            pipe.Writer.Advance(buffer.Length);
            await pipe.Writer.FlushAsync();

            await ReadAndDump();

            async Task ReadAndDump()
            {
                var result = await pipe.Reader.ReadAsync();
                Console.Write("[");
                bool first = true;
                foreach (var segment in result.Buffer)
                {
                    if (!first)
                    {
                        Console.Write(",");
                    }

                    Console.Write(segment.Length);
                    first = false;
                }
                Console.WriteLine("]");
                pipe.Reader.AdvanceTo(result.Buffer.End);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This prints: `[0,8192]`
